<section class="panel" aria-label="Independent Grade 3 unit 5 lesson 4 Secret Fractions source-aligned teaching">
<h2>Build fractions from unit fractions — source-checked teaching</h2>
<p>The original 2021 source teaches a <strong>variable-target card game</strong> and four fixed Pilolo street-fraction situations. These are new first-party displays rather than the publisher's cards, gameboard or photograph.</p>
<h3>Mental warm-up</h3><p>3 × 3 = 9; 7 × 3 = 21; 10 × 3 = 30; 3 × 17 = 51 (30 + 21).</p>
<h3>Secret Fractions — card inventory</h3>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Deck</th><th scope="col">Original mathematics on cards</th><th scope="col">Count</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Unit fractions</th><td>1/2 × 2; 1/3 × 3; 1/4 × 4; 1/6 × 6; 1/8 × 9</td><td>24</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Secret target fractions</th><td>2/2, 2/3, 3/3, 2/4, 3/4, 4/4, 2/6, 4/6, 5/6, 2/8, 3/8, 5/8</td><td>12</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Draw two secret targets. On each turn, take one unit card or exchange both targets. Build a target by collecting unit-fraction copies of the same whole, shade the matching equal-area representation, then draw a replacement target. The first to complete two targets wins; teacher checks gameboard validity and play.</p>
<p>For example, three cards marked 1/4 build 3/4, and five cards marked 1/8 build 5/8. Counts above are the original deck inventory, <strong>not</strong> a claim that all players receive the same cards or answers.</p>
<h3>Pilolo — independent length models</h3><p>Each strip represents one entire street. The location is the end of the shaded part, not an amount of unequal colored pieces. The four story cases are fixed in the public source.</p>
